# 3. Five Common QA Metrics

| QA Metric                       | Definition                                                                                                      | Use in a Paytm-style Payments App                                                                                   |
| ------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **1. Defect Density**           | Number of defects found compared with the size of the software/module.                                          | Helps identify modules with a high number of bugs, such as the payment or recharge module.                          |
| **2. Test Case Execution Rate** | Percentage of planned test cases that have actually been executed.                                              | Helps the QA team know how much of the planned testing has been completed before release.                           |
| **3. Defect Leakage**           | Defects that were missed during testing but were found after release.                                           | Helps measure how effective testing was before a payment feature was released to users.                             |
| **4. Test Coverage**            | Percentage of requirements or functionality covered by test cases.                                              | Helps ensure important features such as UPI, wallet, recharge and transaction history are properly tested.          |
| **5. Defect Reopen Rate**       | Percentage of fixed defects that are reopened because the problem still exists or the fix caused another issue. | A high rate could indicate that developers need to investigate defects more carefully before marking them as fixed. |

### Why Metrics Matter

QA metrics give the team a quick idea about the **quality of the application and progress of testing**. For a payments app, this is especially important because even a small defect can affect transactions and user trust.

